            <ArticleTitle>Late postpartum eclampsia: examples and review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>471-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Eclampsia, defined as the occurrence of seizures in pregnant women, usually in the setting of preeclampsia and in the absence of other neurologic disorders, occurs mainly before, during , or within 48 hours after delivery. When convulsions occur later postpartum, diagnosis is difficult and treatment disputed. We review the entity of late postpartum eclampsia and report 2 examples in which the serum levels of antiangiogenic and angiogenic proteins were measured.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
